Given \(V_{S}=10\,\text{V}\), \(R_{S}=50\,\Omega\), \(V_{Z}=6\,\text{V}\), \(I_{Z(\min)}=5\,\text{mA}\) For the voltage across \(R\) not to fall below \(6\,\text{V}\), the zener diode must remain in breakdown region. Hence the current through the zener diode should satisfy \[I_{Z}\geq 5\,\text{mA}\] Current through the series resistor is \[I=\frac{V_{S}-V_{Z}}{R_{S}} =
\frac{10-6}{50}
=80\,\text{mA}\] This current divides into load current and zener current: \[I=I_{R}+I_{Z}\] For minimum permissible value of \(R\), \(I_{Z}=5\,\text{mA}\) Therefore,\[I_{R}=80-5\] \[I_{R}=75\,\text{mA}\] Now, \[R_{\min}=\frac{V_{Z}}{I_{R}}\] \[R_{\min}=\frac{6}{75\times10^{-3}}=80\,\Omega\] \[\boxed{\text{(B)}\quad 80\,\Omega}\]

Since \(\beta\) is very large, \(I_B=0\) Hence, the voltage across the \(1.65\,\text{k}\Omega\) resistor is obtained using the voltage divider rule. \[V_{1.65}
